## 3. Secrets & Sensitive Data

| Requirement | Standard |
|-------------|----------|
| Storage | Environment variables or secret vaults — never in code, commits, rules, or logs |
| At rest | Secrets and regulated personal data are encrypted at rest. The key lives in the vault |
| Client exposure | No backend URLs, API keys, RBAC matrices, or role names in client bundles |
| Env prefixes | `PUBLIC_*` / `VITE_*` only for intentionally public values — review before merge |
| PII in URLs | User IDs, tokens, and credentials in request **body or headers**, not query strings |
| Logging | Sanitize dynamic log values (`Encode.forJava()` in Java; equivalent elsewhere) |
| DB access | Least-privilege DB users per service; rotate keys per org policy |
| Responses | `@JsonIgnore` on sensitive response fields; no secrets in error bodies, logs, or client bundles |

Encryption in transit is §7. Audit events for this data are §12. Those three clauses are the BSP and Data Privacy Act controls this baseline requires when the system stores regulated personal data.

---

## 4. Cookies & Client Storage

| Attribute | Session / auth cookies |
|-----------|------------------------|
| `HttpOnly` | **Required** |
| `Secure` | **Required** in production |
| `SameSite` | `Lax` or `Strict` |
| Access token | Memory only. Never `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, or `document.cookie` |
| Refresh token | HttpOnly cookie set by the server |
| Cache | Clear in-memory identity, the refresh cookie, and any leftover client storage on logout |

---

## 5. File Uploads

| Check | Required |
|-------|----------|
| Server-side validation | **Always** — client `accept=` is supplemental only |
| Size limit | Explicit per endpoint |
| Content validation | Magic-byte / MIME sniffing + structure parse (e.g. CSV header row) |
| Extension allowlist | Supplemental — not sole check |
| Stored name | Generated. The client file name is not a path. Reject `..` |
| Decompression | Uncompressed-size limit before extraction |
| Authorization | `@PreAuthorize` on all admin upload endpoints |
| Virus scan | Required in production (async queue is acceptable) |

---

## 6. HTTP Security Headers

| Header | Value |
|--------|-------|
| `Strict-Transport-Security` | `max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains` (with HTTPS) |
| `X-Content-Type-Options` | `nosniff` |
| `X-Frame-Options` | `DENY` or `SAMEORIGIN` (second header; CSP `frame-ancestors` is the frame control) |
| `Content-Security-Policy` | Restrict script sources; `frame-ancestors`; `base-uri 'self'`; `object-src 'none'` |
| `Referrer-Policy` | `no-referrer` or `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` |
| `Permissions-Policy` | Set. Disable browser features the app does not use |

`'unsafe-inline'` for scripts is an exception that names the nonce or hash plan.

Remove fingerprinting headers (`X-Powered-By`, `Server` version leaks) where configurable.

**CORS:** explicit allowed origins in production; avoid `*` with credentials.

**CSP / XSS:** define CSP for apps rendering HTML; restrict script sources — see `frontend-web.mdc` for XSS discipline.

---

## 7. API Security

- Versioned paths (`/api/v1/...`)
- Authentication on all non-health endpoints
- TLS 1.2 or 1.3 on every outbound call, with certificate verification
- Rate limiting at edge or BFF for auth, admin, and upload endpoints
- Input validation at API boundary (`@Valid`, Bean Validation)
- Parameterized queries / JPA named parameters only — no SQL string concatenation
- Sort and filter fields come from an allowlist
- A user-supplied URL is fetched only when the host is on an allowlist. Redirects stay on that allowlist
- Appropriate HTTP status codes (401/403/405/406 — not 200 with error body)
- Do not leak stack traces, internal hostnames, or schema details in production errors

---

## 8. Production Hygiene

- `DEBUG` off in production
- No demo/debug routes (`/api/env-var`, `/api/echo`) in prod builds
- Source maps disabled in production client bundles unless explicitly approved
- `app.security.permit-all=false` in all deployed environments

---

## 9. Supply Chain

- Dependency scanning in CI (OWASP Dependency-Check, Snyk, Dependabot) — see `quality-and-testing.mdc`
- No new high or critical CVE without a documented exception
- Lockfiles are committed
- GitLab secret detection runs on the merge request. A committed secret fails the pipeline
- Every service that ships produces a CycloneDX or SPDX SBOM
- Document the license policy when compliance requires it

